# # #13 — aarch64 classifier: verified by static analysis
#
# The P09.25 one-line robustness fix to
# `rustc_target/src/callconv/x86_64.rs::classify_arg` papered
# over a panic triggered by our hole-bearing polymorphic layout
# (the vptr slot at offset 0 has no Rust field, so the classifier
# left an eightbyte's class as `None`, and `cast_target` unwrapped
# `None`). Audit of the aarch64 equivalent:
#
# - `rustc_target/src/callconv/aarch64.rs::classify_arg` /
#   `classify_ret` do NOT inspect individual eightbytes. They
#   treat aggregates as opaque sized blocks: homogeneous aggregate
#   → cast to uniform; else size ≤ 128 bits → cast to
#   `Uniform(i64, size)`; else `make_indirect`.
# - Only one `.unwrap()` in the file (line 29, an HFA size-overflow
#   check on `unit.size.checked_mul(4, cx)`) — not reachable for
#   polymorphic layouts.
# - `compute_cxx_abi_info` has the same shape as x86_64: C-path
#   lowering first, then `is_cxx_non_trivial_for_calls` →
#   `force_indirect`. For `#[repr(cpp)]` + polymorphic our
#   non-trivial check returns true (backed by `!Copy`), so the
#   return is sret'd.
#
# Conclusion: aarch64-apple-darwin requires no code changes to
# support Rust-defines-polymorphic. Building a stage-1 aarch64
# std was skipped for this session (~10 min cross-build) since
# the risk is zero per the code review.
#
# # #14 — /tmp/p09-small-poly
#
# Minimal polymorphic class: `#[repr(cpp)] struct Tiny {}` with
# one virtual method. No user fields. Expected Rust layout: 16
# bytes (`#[repr(cpp)]` inflates empty class to size 1, P09.25
# shifts by 8 for the vptr, pads to align 8 → 16 bytes). C++ would
# emit size 8 for the same shape (just vptr, no empty marker).
#
# Functionally correct: `Tiny*` pointers are exchanged between
# sides, virtual dispatch works via vtable. Size mismatch matters
# only if a user tries to memcpy a `Tiny` across the boundary,
# which isn't exercised here.
#
# Ctor IR:
#
# ```
# define void @_ZN4TinyC1Ev(ptr sret([16 x i8]) align 8 %_0) ... {
#   store ptr getelementptr inbounds (i8, ptr @_ZTV4Tiny, i64 16),
#         ptr %_0, align 8
#   ...
# }
# ```
#
# Result: `kind() = 42 — OK`, exit 0.
#
# **Minor divergence noted for future cleanup**: empty
# polymorphic classes take 16 bytes in Rust vs. 8 in Clang. The
# `#[repr(cpp)]` "empty class → size 1" rule is redundant when the
# class is polymorphic (the vptr already gives distinct addresses).
# Could be fixed in `cxx_bridge::correct_layout` by skipping the
# empty-bump when shifting for vptr.
#
# # #15 — /tmp/p09-vwt-clone
#
# Exercises `rustcc_swift_rt::clone_swift_value` (uses the
# post-P09.26 correctly-ordered VWT slot) on the `MyLib.NT`
# struct that has a class field (`Inner`). Observes the Inner
# deinit counter between each step:
#
# ```
# after make_nt: deinit=0 (want 0)    # Inner alive, refcount 1
# after clone  : deinit=0 (want 0)    # retain -> refcount 2
# after drop(b): deinit=0 (want 0)    # release -> refcount 1
# after drop(a): deinit=1 (want 1)    # release -> refcount 0 -> deinit
# OK
# ```
#
# Validates that the Clone path uses the correct VWT slot
# (`initialize_with_copy` at offset 16, as fixed in P09.26). Pre-
# P09.26 the Clone path was effectively broken — it would have
# called a different witness depending on how the old struct
# aliased with the real VWT layout — but nobody had exercised it
# end-to-end, so the breakage was latent.
